WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:14.360
Let’s get one thing out of the way: water
fleas are not fleas.

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:17.320
For one, fleas are insects.

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:23.060
Water fleas are a subset of small crustaceans
called Cladocera.

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:29.070
There are over 600 known species of Cladocerans,
but scientists have estimated that there might

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:33.880
actually be around 2-4 times that number.

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:41.340
Water fleas got their name because not only
do they, you know, kind of resemble fleas,

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:44.739
they’ve also been known to jump like them.

00:00:44.739 --> 00:00:51.110
And it’s kind of a strange experience to
see something that looks fairly familiar reconfigured

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:54.460
into the microcosmic.

00:00:54.460 --> 00:01:00.309
If you look at Daphnia from the side, you
can see the definite flea-type shape that

00:01:00.309 --> 00:01:03.120
informs their name.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:08.719
But if you manage to look at one from the
top, well I could very easily lie to you and

00:01:08.719 --> 00:01:13.689
say that this is a completely different organism,
and you would probably believe me.

00:01:13.689 --> 00:01:15.709
Now, of course, we would never do that.

00:01:15.709 --> 00:01:18.040
That’s not the kind of show this is.

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:26.579
Water fleas are large in microcosmos terms.

00:01:26.579 --> 00:01:32.899
They range from around 0.5 mm to 6 mm long,
absolutely gigantic.

00:01:32.899 --> 00:01:40.649
And they’ve got 10 sets of appendages distributed
around their body, including 2 different sets

00:01:40.649 --> 00:01:42.170
of antennae.

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:46.619
One of those is for sensing, the other is
for swimming.

00:01:46.619 --> 00:01:53.409
There's also an array of 5-6 limbs that form
a feeding and respiratory apparatus, as well

00:01:53.409 --> 00:01:59.909
as a pair of claws at the end of their abdomen,
which they use as a filtering tool or for

00:01:59.909 --> 00:02:01.460
defense.

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:07.729
What might be especially striking is that
one very large eye, something we don’t often

00:02:07.729 --> 00:02:09.879
see in the microcosmos.

00:02:09.879 --> 00:02:14.819
As embryos, Cladocerans have two brown eye
spots.

00:02:14.819 --> 00:02:20.860
But in the last stage of their development,
the eyespots merge together to form one large

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:27.220
compound eye that will go on to help orient
them while they swim around.

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:34.709
Cladocerans also another small photosensitive
organ called the naupliar eye, which is located

00:02:34.709 --> 00:02:37.790
between the compound eye and the mouth.

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:43.480
The water fleas we see most in our own journey
through the microcosmos are Daphnia, a genus

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:47.040
that includes more than a hundred species.

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:53.329
And while yes, daphnia are found in lakes,
they're also sold in aquarium stories because

00:02:53.329 --> 00:02:56.400
fish love to eat them.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:00.840
You might see bags of them, filled with hundreds
of daphnia.

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:05.380
As they get packed together, the oxygen around
them gets depleted.

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:11.790
Cladocerans use hemoglobin to transport oxygen,
and in situations like this, they can increase

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:19.690
the concentration of hemoglobin in their bodies
up to 20-fold to transport more oxygen.

00:03:19.690 --> 00:03:25.840
And as that happens, you might actually see
those packs of daphnia become a much more

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:27.060
vivid red.

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:35.020
Here, you can see the Daphnia’s heart beating.

00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:40.450
At 20 degrees Celsius, it’ll beat around
200 times a minute.

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:44.989
But that rate depends on the temperature,
it beats slower when the daphnia are in colder

00:03:44.989 --> 00:03:46.019
conditions.

00:03:46.019 --> 00:03:52.110
The heart rate will also change in the presence
of substances like alcohol and caffeine, but

00:03:52.110 --> 00:04:00.180
we’re a little bit too nervous about hurting
our daphnia to demonstrate that.

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:06.930
Our daphnia often come from those aquarium
packs, rescued from their fate as fish-food

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:12.590
to live a less hazardous life in our own fish-free
pond aquarium.

00:04:12.590 --> 00:04:19.650
They do occasionally get eaten by the hydras
in the tank, but it's a better chance of survival

00:04:19.650 --> 00:04:24.370
compared with the alternative.

00:04:24.370 --> 00:04:29.500
Daphnia and their cladoceran relatives are
filter feeders, gathering their food with

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:33.090
a filtering apparatus made of their limbs.

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:40.070
Key among these are their phyllopods, a set
of flat legs that resemble leaves.

00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:46.410
The phyllopods create a water current that
flows from the front of the water flea towards

00:04:46.410 --> 00:04:50.740
the back, allowing it to collect and consume
algae.

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:58.449
Here, you can see a Daphnia filtering a rotifer,
though the rotifer is quickly discarded probably because

00:04:58.449 --> 00:05:03.470
it's probably a bit too big for easy consumption.

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:09.790
While daphnia are often transparent, you can
sometimes get hints of just what food is dominating

00:05:09.790 --> 00:05:15.780
their diet by looking at the tinges of color
that line their body.

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:20.129
With more green algae, the daphnia will start
to look a little green or yellow.

00:05:20.129 --> 00:05:26.860
And when they eat more bacteria, you might
see more of a white or salmon pink color.

00:05:26.860 --> 00:05:29.340
Daphnia are, of course, often eaten.

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:35.479
So, they employ several strategies to avoid
predators, including the tried and true strategy

00:05:35.479 --> 00:05:38.620
of just getting the heck out of the way.

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:43.550
Only when Daphnia do it, we call it diel vertical
migration.

00:05:43.550 --> 00:05:49.509
During the day, when light is plentiful, they
stake out lower, darker depths where predators

00:05:49.509 --> 00:05:51.750
are less able to see them.

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:58.200
During the night, they move upwards, able
to eat without fear of looming threats.

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:05.520
Daphnia arrange this diel vertical migration
around the presence of fish kairomones.

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:10.169
Kairomones are chemical signals that alert
them to the proximity of fish.

00:06:10.169 --> 00:06:14.770
If they don't detect these signals, the Daphnia
might just spend the day hanging out towards

00:06:14.770 --> 00:06:17.680
the surface so they can gather more food.

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:26.750
But kairomones don't just affect the Daphnia's
migration, they also affect the size of their offspring.

00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:33.070
In the presence of fish kairomones, one daphnia
species has been observed birthing smaller

00:06:33.070 --> 00:06:38.740
water fleas, a change in size that helps them
avoid detection.

00:06:38.740 --> 00:06:43.720
Interestingly though, the presence of the
phantom midge, another predator, sets off

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:50.789
the opposite result: their kairomones will
make the Daphnia grow larger, presumably because

00:06:50.789 --> 00:06:54.040
the midge prefers a smaller prey.

00:06:54.040 --> 00:06:58.900
In response to the midge's kairomones, the
Daphnia have also been observed growing a

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:04.560
set of jagged edges on their head called "neck-teeth".

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:13.280
You know, all the usual protective measures,
growing extra large and having teeth on your neck.

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:19.379
Environmental cues also shape the reproduction
of Daphnia by directly affecting the sexual

00:07:19.379 --> 00:07:22.020
composition of their population.

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:27.879
An adult female daphnia will usually go through
asexual reproduction, producing a clutch of

00:07:27.879 --> 00:07:31.889
parthenogenetic eggs every time she molts.

00:07:31.889 --> 00:07:37.229
The eggs go into her brood chamber, and after
about a day, they hatch.

00:07:37.229 --> 00:07:44.189
The young larvae stay inside her brood chamber
for about 3 more days, emerging as young Daphnia

00:07:44.189 --> 00:07:47.470
that are genetically identical to their mother.

00:07:47.470 --> 00:07:54.139
With asexual reproduction, a female Daphnia
might produce a clutch every 3-4 days until

00:07:54.139 --> 00:08:00.229
her death, producing more clones of herself
and rapidly increasing their population.

00:08:00.229 --> 00:08:04.479
There are a few Daphnia species that are strictly
parthenogenetic.

00:08:04.479 --> 00:08:13.879
But most follow a pattern called cyclic parthenogenesis, interrupting their asexual cycle for sexual reproduction.

00:08:13.879 --> 00:08:21.350
This switch happens in response to environmental
cues signaling that times have gotten tough.

00:08:21.350 --> 00:08:26.139
Maybe the population has become too dense
and their food has become scarce.

00:08:26.139 --> 00:08:32.510
Or perhaps the days are getting shorter, the
temperatures lower, or the pond drier.

00:08:32.510 --> 00:08:38.460
When these external danger signals are sensed,
some of the parthenogenetically-derived eggs

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:41.330
will start to hatch into males.

00:08:41.330 --> 00:08:46.640
These male Daphnia are genetically identical
to their female brood mates, after all they

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:49.520
are parthenogenetically produced.

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:55.540
And that means that sex in daphnia is an environmentally determined trait.

00:08:55.540 --> 00:09:00.880
There isn’t a genetic difference between
males and females, rather there’s some kind

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:07.340
of external signal that takes a genetically
identical population of daphnia and determines

00:09:07.340 --> 00:09:09.990
whether some of them will become male.

00:09:09.990 --> 00:09:15.300
Those males then reproduce with females from
other clutches, creating an opportunity for

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:18.020
true gene exchange.

00:09:18.020 --> 00:09:23.050
Following sexual reproduction, the female
daphnia will produce a protective saddle-like

00:09:23.050 --> 00:09:29.680
structure called an ephippium, which holds
two eggs called the resting eggs.

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:33.630
When the female molts, she will shed the ephippium
too.

00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:38.700
It might sink to the bottom of the pond or
disperse through the water or with the wind.

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:43.350
And where it lands, it might rest for years.

00:09:43.350 --> 00:09:48.860
It's only when the environment that surrounds
the egg signals favorable conditions that

00:09:48.860 --> 00:09:55.960
they will hatch, producing females that will
soon proceed back to their normal parthenogenetic

00:09:55.960 --> 00:09:58.160
reproduction schedule.

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:01.170
Nature is full of change.

00:10:01.170 --> 00:10:08.050
Light, temperature, your neighbors--these
can alter dramatically over the course of

00:10:08.050 --> 00:10:10.520
days, weeks, and years.

00:10:10.520 --> 00:10:16.290
And so, the life of any organism, from the
smallest microbe to the largest whale, is

00:10:16.290 --> 00:10:22.080
built on responding and adapting to the changes
around them.

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:27.190
Compared to many of their smaller neighbors,
water fleas don’t look like they would be

00:10:27.190 --> 00:10:30.680
the most nimble in the face of existential
threat.

00:10:30.680 --> 00:10:38.050
In our own footage, they often stand out like
awkward giants, straddling the thin line that

00:10:38.050 --> 00:10:41.630
separates the world of the micro and macro.

00:10:41.630 --> 00:10:47.880
How can an organism that doesn’t quite seem
to fit in either world contend with the challenges

00:10:47.880 --> 00:10:49.570
of both?

00:10:49.570 --> 00:10:51.190
Quite well, apparently.

00:10:51.190 --> 00:10:56.430
It’s as if they have managed to make the
best of both worlds, mastering the reproductive

00:10:56.430 --> 00:11:01.130
strategies of one while processing the signals
of the other.

00:11:01.130 --> 00:11:06.860
Yes, it is true, water fleas may not be the
most elegant of the

00:11:06.860 --> 00:11:09.780
creatures we see in the microcosmos.

00:11:09.780 --> 00:11:15.940
But with a bag full of ingenious tricks, you
can’t deny that they’ve got style.

00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:23.280
Thanks for coming on this journey with us
as we explore

00:11:23.290 --> 00:11:25.770
the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:11:25.770 --> 00:11:30.230
If you like this, well, that makes us really
happy.

00:11:30.230 --> 00:11:33.910
And if you want to thank somebody, you can
thank us, but you can also thank all of the

00:11:33.910 --> 00:11:35.670
people on this screen right now.

00:11:35.670 --> 00:11:38.040
They are our patrons on Patreon.

00:11:38.040 --> 00:11:45.930
They are the reason we are able to make this
fun show in these uncertain times of constant change.

00:11:45.930 --> 00:11:50.860
If you want to see more from our Master of
Microscopes, James, check out Jam &amp; Germs

00:11:50.860 --> 00:11:52.440
on Instagram.

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:58.400
And if you want to see more from us, we are
